  "source": {
    "display_title": "Compiler-Aided DNA Strand Displacement Circuits",
    "authors": [
      "Anupama J. Thubagere",
      "Chris Thachuk",
      "Joseph Berleant",
      "Robert F. Johnson",
      "Diana A. Ardelean",
      "Kevin M. Cherry",
      "Lulu Qian"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-seesaw-compiler-automates-dna-circuit-design--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "The Seesaw Compiler automates the translation of feedforward digital logic into equivalent dual-rail and seesaw DNA strand displacement circuits, while producing simulation code and DNA sequences for construction.",
      "short_title": "Compiler automates DNA circuits",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "A feedforward digital logic circuit is first translated into an equivalent dual-rail logic circuit and then translated into an equivalent seesaw DNA circuit. Visual DSD code and Mathematica code are generated for analysing and simulating the seesaw DNA circuit, and DNA sequences are generated for constructing the circuit."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-rule-110-124-circuit-has-78-species--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "The compiled rule 110-124 DNA strand displacement circuit contains six layers and 78 distinct initial DNA species.",
      "short_title": "Circuit has 78 species",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The DNA circuit generated by the Seesaw Compiler consisted of 6 layers and a total of 78 distinct initial DNA species."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-unpurified-strands-can-build-complex-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "Unpurified DNA strands can be used to construct complex DNA strand displacement circuits when circuit design software is combined with simplified experimental procedures.",
      "short_title": "Unpurified strands support circuits",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Here we demonstrate the use of circuit design software, combined with the use of unpurified strands and simplified experimental procedures, for creating a complex DNA strand displacement circuit that consists of 78 distinct species."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-effective-concentration-calibration-guides-thresholds--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "Effective concentration calibration provides quantitative threshold choices that restore desired OR and AND gate behavior for unpurified seesaw components.",
      "short_title": "Calibration guides gate thresholds",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Using b/a = 1.4, we chose a nominal threshold of 0.35x and 0.85x for two-input OR and AND gate, respectively, and 0.4x and 1.6x for three-input OR and AND gate. Experiments of the logic gates showed desired behaviours."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-outlier-gates-expose-synthesis-variation--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "Gate calibration can identify outlier unpurified components whose effective concentrations differ enough to disrupt multi-layer circuit composition.",
      "short_title": "Outliers disrupt gate composition",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "With a gate calibration experiment shown in Fig. 4b, we measured that g18,53/a18,53 = 0.8x for one gate and g22,53/a22,53 = 0.44x for another."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-systematic-flow-shortens-unpurified-circuit-building--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "A three-step systematic procedure of calibration, outlier adjustment, and output tuning enabled construction of the rule 110 sub-circuit in three days.",
      "short_title": "Procedure shortens circuit building",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Starting from the calibration of effective concentrations for threshold and gate species in general, to the identification and adjustment of any outliers, and then to the final tuning of circuit output, we established three sequential steps for building unpurified seesaw circuits.",
        "Following the flowchart, we completed the construction of the rule 110 sub-circuit in only 3 days."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-full-circuit-recovers-ideal-ca-patterns--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "The full rule 110-124 DNA circuit produced dual-rail fluorescence outputs at 24 hours that visually matched the intended cellular automata pattern relationships.",
      "short_title": "Outputs match automata patterns",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The arrays for the DNA circuit were plotted using the output values at 24 h from the data. The ideal logic circuit behaviour corresponds to an image of a black dog with a white background for R1241, an inverted image for R1240 and their mirror images for R1101 and R1100, respectively."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-synthesis-error-model-explains-delayed-outputs--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "A model that represents synthesis errors in unpurified molecules predicts the slower 24-hour behavior of the rule 110-124 circuit better than the purified-circuit model.",
      "short_title": "Error model explains delay",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Simulations of the rule 110-124 circuit using the previously developed model for purified seesaw circuits predicted desired outputs in roughly 8 h and undesired reactions taking over in 24 h, while simulations using the new model for unpurified seesaw circuits predicted desired outputs in roughly 24 h."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "claim-signal-restoration-compensates-for-impurity--367a566abb69",
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "text": "Signal restoration and component tuning compensate for impurity in unpurified seesaw circuits, although leak reactions and sensitivity to synthesis and stoichiometry errors remain limiting concerns.",
      "short_title": "Restoration compensates impurity",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The signal restoration built in to every logic gate allows simple tuning to restore desired circuit behaviour, compensating for the impurity of molecules.",
        "It is also necessary to eliminate leak reactions in DNA circuits and to improve the building blocks such that they are substantially less sensitive to synthesis errors and stoichiometry errors."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-abstract-78-species--7ad8ad2fba3b",
      "title": "Unpurified 78-Species Circuit",
      "excerpt": "Here we demonstrate the use of circuit design software, combined with the use of unpurified strands and simplified experimental procedures, for creating a complex DNA strand displacement circuit that consists of 78 distinct species. We develop a systematic procedure for overcoming the challenges involved in using unpurified DNA strands.",
      "overview": "This abstract-level result establishes the scale and experimental premise of the work: a large DNA strand displacement circuit is not merely designed computationally but constructed using unpurified components. It supports the source’s accessibility claim while leaving the detailed validation to the calibration, tuning, implementation, and modeling evidence elsewhere in the paper.",
      "evidence_type": "observational_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1",
        "2"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-compiler-translation-pipeline--d0e184d4f09a",
      "title": "Compiler Translation Pipeline",
      "excerpt": "A feedforward digital logic circuit is first translated into an equivalent dual-rail logic circuit and then translated into an equivalent seesaw DNA circuit. Visual DSD code and Mathematica code are generated for analysing and simulating the seesaw DNA circuit, and DNA sequences are generated for constructing the circuit.",
      "overview": "The figure caption gives a concrete formal workflow for the compiler rather than a generic software claim. It matters because the paper’s experimental construction depends on automated movement from high-level logic to molecular species, simulation artifacts, and constructible DNA sequences. The evidence does not prove all compiler outputs are correct in general, but it grounds the claimed design path for the demonstrated circuit.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-rule-110-124-six-layers--367a566abb69",
      "title": "Six-Layer Rule Circuit",
      "excerpt": "The DNA circuit generated by the Seesaw Compiler consisted of 6 layers and a total of 78 distinct initial DNA species. The circuit was designed to compute a combined logic function of the rule 110 and rule 124 transition rules.",
      "overview": "This design evidence fixes the circuit’s functional target and molecular scale. Rule 110 and rule 124 provide a recognizable nontrivial logic benchmark, while six layers and 78 initial species show that the experiment goes beyond one- or two-component demonstrations. The evidence supports claims about the particular circuit’s complexity, not arbitrary scalability to all molecular programs.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-threshold-calibration-values--7ad8ad2fba3bd",
      "title": "Threshold Calibration Values",
      "excerpt": "Using b/a = 1.4, we chose a nominal threshold of 0.35x and 0.85x for two-input OR and AND gate, respectively, and 0.4x and 1.6x for three-input OR and AND gate. Experiments of the logic gates showed desired behaviours.",
      "overview": "These numerical threshold choices are a concrete calibration artifact. They show how unpurified threshold and signal concentrations are not assumed ideal but are measured and folded into gate design. The values directly support the paper’s claim that systematic calibration can recover desired OR and AND behavior, although they remain tied to the strands, provider, procedure, and batch conditions used in the study.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-outlier-gate-ratios--d0e184d4f09af",
      "title": "Outlier Gate Ratios",
      "excerpt": "With a gate calibration experiment shown in Fig. 4b, we measured that g18,53/a18,53 = 0.8x for one gate and g22,53/a22,53 = 0.44x for another. The output wires of the two upstream OR gates were significantly different from each other.",
      "overview": "The contrasting gate ratios document a specific failure mode in composing unpurified parts: nominally comparable gates can release signals at materially different effective concentrations. This evidence motivates the outlier-identification step and constrains the overall conclusion, because successful construction depends on detecting and compensating for such sequence- or synthesis-dependent variation.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-three-step-flowchart--367a566abb690",
      "title": "Three-Step Build Procedure",
      "excerpt": "Starting from the calibration of effective concentrations for threshold and gate species in general, to the identification and adjustment of any outliers, and then to the final tuning of circuit output, we established three sequential steps for building unpurified seesaw circuits. Following the flowchart, we completed the construction of the rule 110 sub-circuit in only 3 days.",
      "overview": "The flowchart evidence turns the experimental adjustments into a reproducible procedure. It establishes that the authors did not rely on ad hoc luck after observing impurity effects: they sequenced calibration, outlier correction, and tuning. The three-day result is a practical benchmark for this sub-circuit, not a universal time guarantee for all circuits.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "5"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-full-circuit-24h-output--7ad8ad2fba3bd2",
      "title": "Full Circuit 24-Hour Output",
      "excerpt": "Figure 7 reports fluorescence kinetics data of the two pairs of dual-rail outputs. The arrays for the DNA circuit were plotted using the output values at 24 h from the data, and the ideal logic circuit behaviour corresponds to a black dog image for R1241, an inverted image for R1240, and mirror images for R1101 and R1100.",
      "overview": "This figure-level evidence connects molecular fluorescence measurements to the intended cellular automata visualization. It matters because the paper’s design and tuning pipeline culminates in the full rule 110-124 circuit, not only isolated gates or sub-circuits. The comparison is qualitative and visual, so it supports successful pattern-level computation rather than exact analog agreement at every time point.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "6"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-unpurified-model-timing--d0e184d4f09af6",
      "title": "Unpurified Model Timing",
      "excerpt": "Simulations of the rule 110-124 circuit using the previously developed model for purified seesaw circuits predicted that the circuit should yield desired outputs in roughly 8 h and the undesired reactions will take over in 24 h. Simulations using the new model for unpurified seesaw circuits predicted that the circuit should yield desired outputs in roughly 24 h.",
      "overview": "The timing comparison is a concrete modeling result showing why a purified-circuit kinetic expectation is misleading for unpurified strands. By incorporating synthesis-error populations, the new model aligns with the experimentally relevant slower output time. The evidence supports semi-quantitative explanatory power, while the source itself still frames future robustness improvements as necessary.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-05-compiler-aided-systematic-construction-of-large-scale-dna-strand-displacement-circuits--367a566abb69",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig.9"
      ]
    }
  ],
